if they've never been to Copenhagen  people's primary association with Danish  capital might be the statue of the  little mermaid in the harbor but the  most beautiful sight is the city itself  with its unique seaside location this  year British lifestyle magazine monocle  and its editor-in-chief Tyler Brule  placed Copenhagen at number six in its  ranking of the world's most livable  cities when we look at Copenhagen one of  the things which I think has forced a  bit of a stumble comes back to to  connectivity public transport they've  done a great job of course is is it the  global home of the bicycle bike paths  here almost as wide as street lanes for  cars Copenhagen is one of the world's  most bicycle-friendly cities and cycling  is part of everyday life the city has  more bicycles than residents every  second person right to bike to work here  footrests a traffic light sees the  waiting and tilted trash cans were  designed specially for cyclists  Copenhagen of course continues to  succeed because they have the bicycle  miracle in the city it is just it's so  easy to get from A to B to see it on to  Zed just by going around on two wheels  or potentially three wheels if you have  if you have children  it's an incredibly walkable city but  just over half a million people live  here Copenhagen's moderate-sized makes  it a pretty relaxed metropolis the  cityscape is characterized by  Architecture from the 18th century like  a Malian borg palace for city residents  of the Danish royal family classical  residential and commercial buildings  stand alongside modern constructions  like the opera house new influences are  unmistakable in the restaurant scene too  [Music]  Copenhagen has experienced in the last  five or six years such a boom from a  culinary perspective mean it's really  recognized as probably one of the most  important food cities in the world New  Nordic cuisine began in 2003 when the  restaurant Noma opened here star chef  Rene Redzepi prepared his dishes solely  with regional ingredients Noma was  recognized as the world's best  restaurant four times since then Danish  cooks have been combining Denmark's best  culinary traditions with dishes from  abroad for example at the Royal smooshy  cafe where little works of art are  created on a tiny slice of bread the  smooshy are as handy to eat as sushi and  are reminiscent of smear Brut the Danish  variant of the open-faced sandwich  smooshy are just one Copenhagen success  story what we really like about  Copenhagen is startup means your  restaurant means starting up your own  shop it means launching your own fashion  label doing things with your own hands  and that's one of the great things that  you've seen a lot of people coming back  into the city who are not speculating  they're not looking to build a business  and then just flip it three years later  for some crazy valuation they they  really want to be be part of the urban  fabric  all over the downtown area our little  design shops like henrique vibe scoffs  after studying in London he returned to  Copenhagen who launched his own fashion  label his designs have even been  displayed in museums like New York's  MoMA Copenhagen also stands for design  classics like Ana Jakob Sims furniture  which is still produced today in the  hotel he designed you can even book a  night in the honor Jakob son suite  furnished with original pieces the  capital city of design as Copenhagen  likes to call itself has its share of  planning mistakes of course monocle  magazine says there why Copenhagen only  made it to sixth place on this year's  list of the world's most livable cities  we've seen extraordinary growth within  the city a lot of new development but a  lot of new development which is not very  sympathetic to the rest of the built  environment so quite interesting  buildings architecture away but we see a  lot of buildings which have no  relationship to the street  despite this criticism residents still  regard Copenhagen as Europe's most  pleasant capital with so many people and  music and so colorful so really enjoying  it there's always happy people and in  the summertime here this is a special  place to be  monocle magazine says reducing the 25%  value-added tax in restaurants would be  yet one more way Copenhagen could  improve its already outstanding quality  of life  [Music]
 

